<TopicPage slug="subjects/religious-studies/intermediate/studying-religion-academically">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The academic study of religion is <strong>not believing, debunking or preaching — it is
    comparative, historical and social-scientific analysis</strong> of one of humanity's most
    powerful forces. Its rules: study what traditions do (not just what they claim), respect
    insider perspectives as data, and keep truth-questions open rather than settled.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the scholar's toolkit: dimensions, perspectives, plurality, agnosticism" widget="equationStep" config={methodCfg} />

  <h2>Definitions — and why they fight</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Substantive definitions</strong> (religion = belief in supernatural beings, Tylor) exclude Buddhism-like traditions; <strong>functional definitions</strong> (religion = whatever functions as a person's ultimate concern, Tillich) include nationalism and football. Every definition includes and excludes something — that's the finding.</li>
    <li><strong>The category itself is modern:</strong> "religion" as a separate sphere alongside "politics" and "economics" is a Protestant-era European invention (Wilfred Cantwell Smith's argument) — many cultures never separated them.</li>
    <li><strong>Why it matters:</strong> census categories, legal protections and conflicts all depend on the definitions — defining "religion" is itself an act of power (France's sects lists, India's caste-religion entanglement).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Smart, Dimensions of the Sacred (1996); Cantwell Smith, The Meaning and End of Religion (1962); Nongbri, Before Religion (2013).</p>
</TopicPage>
